{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Jira Server / Data Center support via a Jira-family seam (not a forked second adapter)\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nCMS and similar federal clients run **self-hosted Jira Data Center**, which rebar cannot reconcile against.\nDC differs from Cloud on three axes: REST v2 with wiki-markup/plain-text bodies instead of v3 + ADF, Personal\nAccess Token bearer auth instead of the ACLI subprocess, and name-based user identity instead of `accountId`.\n\nGitHub PR #120 proposed a second adapter package for this. It rides ADR 0035's `Backend` port correctly, but it\nreaches into `adapters/jira/` privates (`_map_local_to_jira_fields`, `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_STATUS`,\n`_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_PRIORITY`, `_sanitize_label`, `_RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK`, `classify_probe_response`) and forks\n`map_fields_to_remote` as a verbatim copy of `adapters/jira/backend.py:42-72` with one line changed. That\ncreates a de facto \"Jira family\" layer with no contract: `adapters/jira/` becomes simultaneously a concrete\nbackend and a shared library, with nothing pinning what is shared and nothing stopping a Cloud change from\nbreaking DC.\n\n## Approach\n\nMake the Jira family an **explicit sub-seam** rather than an accident, in an order that never puts the\nlive-validated Cloud path at risk.\n\n1. **Freeze Cloud first.** Extend the existing held-out characterization tests\n   (`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_backend_characterization.py`) to pin the current outputs of every unit\n   about to move, so any behavior change during extraction fails a pinned oracle.\n2. **Extract a `adapters/jira_family/` shared layer** — and re-point the Cloud adapter at it. Cloud's ACLI\n   transport is not touched at all.\n\n   **Only non-location-pinned symbols may physically move.** ADR 0035 §(a) pins `adf.py`,\n   `outbound_fields.py`, and `comment_limits.py` as **Jira-coupled *and* location-pinned**: they are loaded by\n   filename relative to `_PACKAGE_DIR` (via `_loader.lazy_load(` / `_load_sibling_keyed(` /\n   `spec_from_file_location(`), so physically moving them breaks the dynamic loader unless the loader is taught\n   the new sub-package in the same change — deferred to ADR 0035's Phase 2, explicitly **out of scope here**.\n   Concretely, of the six symbols PR #120 reached for:\n   - **Movable to `jira_family/` in this epic:** `_sanitize_label` and `_RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK` (both in\n     `adapters/jira/jira_fields.py`, not location-pinned), `classify_probe_response`\n     (`adapters/jira/probe.py`, not location-pinned), the priority/status value maps that live in\n     `jira_fields.py`, and the new `RichTextCodec` / `UserIdentityModel` contract objects.\n   - **Stays put:** `_map_local_to_jira_fields`, `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_STATUS`, `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_PRIORITY` — all in the\n     pinned `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py`, and the ADF encoder in the pinned `adapters/jira/adf.py`.\n     These are shared **by dependency-inverting instead of moving**: the pinned module is parameterized by the\n     `jira_family` contracts (it imports *from* `jira_family/`, never the reverse), so DC reuses one\n     implementation without any file relocating and without the loader changing. Any story that would move a\n     pinned file must instead stop and record the blocker.\n3. **Pin the two real axes of variation as contracts**: a `RichTextCodec` (ADF for Cloud, wiki/plain text for\n   DC) and a `UserIdentityModel` (`accountId` for Cloud, `name` for DC). Cloud is parameterized by its existing\n   behavior; DC is a different configuration, not a fork.\n4. **DC transport builds on the maintained `pycontribs/jira` library**, gated behind a `[jira-datacenter]`\n   extra and imported lazily, so the engine's `dependencies = []` contract and the battle-tested Cloud path are\n   both untouched.\n5. **Verify against a real Jira.** Atlassian stopped issuing DC trial licenses on 30 March 2026, so the oracle\n   is a Dockerized Jira from the Atlassian SDK at `localhost:2990` — the same approach pycontribs/jira itself\n   uses in its `tests/conftest.py`.\n6. **Keep the new boundary structurally enforced, not conventional.** The drift this epic exists to prevent can\n   recur the moment a contributor adds Cloud-specific logic to the shared layer. So the seam ships with a\n   machine-checked import contract (an `import-linter` contract in `pyproject.toml`, or an equivalent CI grep\n   check): only `adapters/jira/` and `adapters/jira_datacenter/` may import `adapters/jira_family/`, and\n   `jira_family/` may import neither of them.\n7. **Security posture for the DC connection.** Self-hosted DC is frequently served over plain HTTP inside\n   corporate networks, which would put ticket bodies and the PAT on the wire in cleartext. The DC backend\n   therefore **requires `https`** for `reconciler.base_url` and rejects a non-TLS URL unless an explicit\n   `allow_insecure = true` override is set (which logs a warning). Operator docs state the least-privilege\n   expectation: a **dedicated service account** whose PAT is scoped to only the projects rebar reconciles\n   (Browse Projects / Create Issues / Edit Issues / Add Comments / Link Issues on those projects), never an\n   admin-level token.\n\n**Provenance decision.** DC and Cloud share the single `jira` creation channel, `jira-` local-id prefix, and\n`jira_key` binding; deployments are disambiguated by the `RemoteRef.instance` field that ADR 0035 §(d) already\ndefines for exactly this. They are the same vendor. Generalizing the store vocabulary is epic `be74`'s work,\nwhere a genuinely different vendor (GitHub) makes it necessary; doing it here would mean a store migration for\nno user-visible benefit.\n\n**Alternative rejected: PR #120's separate adapter package.** Two copies of the field maps and sanitizers drift,\nand cross-adapter private imports are a dependency direction ADR 0035 never sanctioned.\n\n**Alternative rejected: a hand-rolled stdlib REST client.** PR #120's client is competent, but pycontribs/jira\nalready absorbs DC-vs-Cloud auth, pagination, and payload quirks that thousands of users have found. Confining\nit to an opt-in extra keeps the cost off every rebar install.\n\n**Alternative rejected: `atlassian-python-api`.** It was surveyed in ADR 0004 and does support DC with PAT\nauth, but `pycontribs/jira` is the better fit here for two reasons: it ships the Dockerized-Jira-SDK test\nharness pattern this epic adopts as its verification oracle (its own `tests/conftest.py`), and its\n`JIRA(server=…, token_auth=…)` surface covers DC-vs-Cloud divergence behind one client object. Either would\nwork; picking one and confining it to an opt-in extra is what matters.\n\n**Alternative rejected: generalizing `jira_key` / the `jira-` prefix / the channel vocabulary now.** That is\nepic `be74`'s spine and touches 50+ binding-store sites; pulling it forward delays the CMS delivery for no\nbehavioral gain.\n\n**Alternative rejected: moving the location-pinned modules now.** Relocating `outbound_fields.py` / `adf.py`\ninto `jira_family/` would be the tidier file layout, but it requires teaching the dynamic loader the new\nsub-package and re-targeting every `mock.patch(\"rebar_reconciler.<mod>.<attr>\")` site in the suite (ADR 0035\n§(a)–(b)). That is ADR 0035's Phase 2; doing it here would couple a live-Cloud-risking refactor to a delivery\nepic for no DC-visible benefit. Dependency inversion gets the same single-implementation guarantee at a\nfraction of the risk.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_family/` (new), `adapters/jira/`, `adapters/jira_datacenter/` (new).\n- `src/rebar/_config_schema.py` — widen `reconciler.backend`, add the TLS validator.\n- `pyproject.toml` — `[jira-datacenter]` extra, and the import contract.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_backend_characterization.py`, `test_backend_contract.py`.\n- A new ADR amending ADR 0035.\n- **Out of scope:** relocating any location-pinned module (`adf.py`, `outbound_fields.py`,\n  `comment_limits.py`) or touching `_loader.py` — ADR 0035 Phase 2 owns that.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] every child story is closed and its change is `Verified +1` on Gerrit\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a full reconcile pass runs green against the Dockerized Jira with `reconciler.backend = \"jira-datacenter\"`,\n      output attached as a comment on the assembly story\n- [ ] `test_backend_contract.py`'s backend fixture includes `jira-datacenter` and the suite passes for it\n- [ ] `pip install nava-rebar` followed by `pip list` shows no `jira` package, and importing rebar still works\n- [ ] an ADR records the Jira-family sub-seam, the shared `jira` provenance identity, and the DC EOL horizon\n- [ ] [operator-attested] rebar reconciles bidirectionally against the Dockerized Jira SDK instance at\n      `localhost:2990`, selected by `reconciler.backend = \"jira-datacenter\"` with `reconciler.base_url`\n      pointing at that instance: at least one issue created in DC appears in the local store after an inbound\n      pass, and one local edit appears on the DC issue after an outbound pass, with zero unhandled exceptions\n      in the reconcile log\n- [ ] No mapping logic exists in two places: the field/value maps, the label sanitizer, the link-relation\n      vocabulary, the probe classifier, the rich-text codec, and the user-identity model each have exactly one\n      implementation reachable by both backends — `adapters/jira_datacenter/` defines none of its own and\n      imports each from `adapters/jira_family/` (or, for the location-pinned modules, consumes the pinned\n      module parameterized by a `jira_family` contract). Enforced by the machine-checked import contract, which\n      fails CI on a violation\n- [ ] The Cloud adapter's observable behavior is unchanged: characterization oracles pinning every unit that\n      moves or is re-pointed were committed **before** the extraction change was pushed for review (the oracle\n      change's Gerrit number recorded on the extraction story), and the full oracle set passes against the\n      post-extraction Cloud adapter\n- [ ] `JiraDataCenterBackend` is exercised by the backend-agnostic contract suite, not only by adapter-local tests.\n- [ ] Installing rebar without the `[jira-datacenter]` extra pulls in no new dependency.\n- [ ] No location-pinned module (`adf.py`, `outbound_fields.py`, `comment_limits.py`) changed path, and\n      `_loader.py` is untouched — verified by `git diff --stat origin/main` on the landed stack\n- [ ] The DC backend rejects a non-`https` `reconciler.base_url` unless `allow_insecure = true` is set, and\n      warns when it is; the operator docs state the least-privilege service-account/PAT scope"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785288431972298001,"uuid":"f26f1e56-2240-41c3-8ee3-4fc685e95f3f"}